tests/*.png

# Bulk data inputs for one-off loaders (DEFRA GeoTIFF ~500 MB, NSPL CSV ~250 MB).
# See scripts/load_defra_raster.py for download URLs and runbook.
# Use data/* (not data/) so the un-ignore below for borough-extra.json
# actually wins; `data/` ignores the dir itself, defeating the negation.
data/*
# F-Perf-10: small borough-metadata JSON IS checked in (extracted from
# index.html for lazy-loading). Everything else under data/ stays ignored.
!data/borough-extra.json
# visual fix-DEFRA-5: aircraft noise PNG cached on our origin, served from
# CloudFront edge. Refreshes via scripts/refresh_aircraft_noise.sh whenever
# DEFRA publishes a new Round of strategic noise mapping.
!data/aircraft-noise-london-lden.png
# London borough boundaries (123 KB), generated by
# scripts/build_london_boroughs.py. Checked in because init() awaits it before
# first paint — fetching it from raw.githubusercontent.com meant a 19.2 MB
# third-party download gated the app rendering at all. Regenerate when the ONS
# LAD vintage rolls, and bump sw.js VERSION in the same commit.
!data/london-boroughs.json
# NYC borough boundaries (238 KB), generated by
# scripts/build_nyc_boroughs.mjs. Checked in for the same reason as London and
# one sharper one: sw.js precaches it, and cache.addAll() is atomic, so if this
# file is missing from the deploy the service worker fails to install at all —
# taking offline support for BOTH cities with it. Regenerate when the source
# vintage rolls, and bump sw.js VERSION in the same commit.
!data/nyc-boroughs.json
# Greater Manchester borough boundaries (66 KB), from the spike branch's ONS
# LAD extract. Checked in for the same reason as the two above, and the failure
# mode without it is quiet rather than loud: `data/*` ignores this directory, so
# a new city's boundaries are invisible to git by DEFAULT. The file sits on the
# author's disk, the map renders, every local check passes - and a fresh clone
# or a deploy shows "Greater Manchester borough outlines could not be loaded".
# Any future city needs a line here.
!data/manchester-boroughs.json
# Locator inset silhouette: England & Wales as one pre-rendered SVG path plus
# the ten core-city marker positions. NO GENERATOR SCRIPT EXISTS - it is a
# checked-in artefact, which is exactly why it must be in git rather than on one
# machine. The region label travels inside the file so the caption follows the
# clip if coverage ever changes.
!data/uk-locator.json
# USA silhouette for the New York view, added 2026-08-10. Same rule as the UK
# one: deliberately NOT in sw.js SHELL_ASSETS, because cache.addAll() is atomic
# and a decoration must not be able to stop the service worker installing.
!data/usa-locator.json

# Core Cities boundary files, added 2026-08-10. `data/*` above ignores this
# directory and every file is un-ignored one at a time, so a new city's
# boundaries are invisible to git BY DEFAULT - it works on the machine that
# generated them, every local gate passes, and the deploy serves "outlines
# could not be loaded". That is exactly how Greater Manchester nearly shipped.
# Regenerate any of these with `python scripts/build_city_boroughs.py --all`.
!data/westmidlands-boroughs.json
!data/westyorkshire-boroughs.json
!data/southyorkshire-boroughs.json
!data/merseyside-boroughs.json
!data/tyneandwear-boroughs.json
!data/bristol-boroughs.json
!data/nottingham-boroughs.json
!data/cardiff-boroughs.json
!data/leicester-boroughs.json
!data/teesside-boroughs.json
.defra_load_checkpoint
# Trailing * is load-bearing since the loader gained --geotiff: checkpoints are
# now namespaced per raster (.defra_load_checkpoint_defra_lden_2022_manchester),
# and the bare pattern above matches only the exact old filename.
.defra_load_checkpoint_*
# Loader resume state (plus its .tmp sibling from the atomic write). Committing
# one is actively dangerous: this repo syncs only via git, so another device
# would silently resume a load it never ran and then stamp __META__ for it.
.nspl_load_checkpoint*
# The air-quality loader's resume state, and the list of postcodes whose write
# could not be made to land. Neither was ignored until 2026-08-09, so the
# checkpoint had been sitting in `git status` as an untracked file for days -
# harmless in itself, but it is exactly the noise that hides a real one.
.defra_aq_checkpoint
.defra_aq_failures

# Raw browser-saved property pages used to derive extension fixtures.
# Ctrl+S output is ~1 MB plus a _files/ directory of assets, none of which the
# extraction reads. The DERIVED fixture (tests/fixtures/rightmove-real-*.html)
# IS committed: it carries the __PAGE_MODEL script verbatim, which is the only
# part any strategy touches, at a tenth the size. Regenerate a new one by
# saving a listing here and trimming to <title>, <h1> and that script.
# Ignore EVERYTHING here, then un-ignore the curated fixtures by name.
#
# This was `tests/fixtures/*bedroom*`, which matched the first raw save only
# because that listing happened to be "81 bedroom block of apartments...".
# Chrome names the file after the page TITLE, so the next two saves - an agent
# branch page and "2 bedroom apartment for rent..." - were a coin flip: one
# matched, one landed in `git status` as a 923 KB untracked file. A pattern
# keyed on someone else's page title is a coincidence waiting to lapse.
#
# Inverting it makes the safe case the default: a raw save is ignored whatever
# it is called, and a fixture only enters git by being named rightmove-*.html,
# which is a deliberate act.
tests/fixtures/*
!tests/fixtures/rightmove-*.html

# stderr from the detached DEFRA air-quality loader (see scripts/load_status.sh)
aqload.err
aqload.log
roadfetch.log
floodfetch.log
